AB  - Fluctuations in operating conditions and unforeseen faults cause cross-domain shifts in fault features and often result in only a partial overlap of fault classesThese challenges significantly degrade the performance of conventional diagnostic models. To address this limitation, we propose a Cross-domain Intelligent Fault Diagnosis Method for Rotating Machinery Based on Deep Universal Domain Adaptation (FDUDA). Specifically, our framework integrates an ensemble of stochastic binary networks as classifiers, modeling the prediction outputs for each fault category with a Gaussian distribution. Meanwhile, the framework adaptively generates confidence scores for shared-class samples to aid in the discrimination of private classes. Building upon this framework, we introduce a soft consistency regularization technique to mitigate the risks posed by private classes across domains. Furthermore, a positive-weighted adversarial training strategy and a Shared-class Local Maximum Mean Discrepancy (SLMMD) method are developed to achieve fine-grained alignment of feature distributions for shared classes in class-uncertainty domains. Finally, two rotating machinery datasets are employed to conduct thorough comparative experiments and ablation studies. The results demonstrate that FDUDA is applicable to cross-domain fault diagnosis under class uncertainty and can effectively identify private fault classes.
