AB  - With the rapid development of information technology and multimedia technology, user-generated content (UGC) videos have been widely shared by users and have become an indispensable part. The quality assessment of UGC videos has become an important topic. Aiming at the problems existing in the insufficient extraction of video frame information and the understanding of adjacent frame relationships, this paper proposes a no-reference video quality assessment (VQA) model based on spatio-temporal perception information. This model is mainly divided into four major parts: spatial domain feature extraction, motion feature extraction, time series modeling and quality prediction. The spatial feature extraction part extracts the spatial feature information of each frame image in different color spaces. While extracting the spatial domain features, the motion information in time series is supplemented by calculating the Euclidean distance on the pixel points of adjacent frames in the video sequence. Finally, the spatial domain features and motion features are fused. The Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) is used to conduct temporal modeling of the fused features, and the quality score of the video is predicted through a fully connected layer dimension reduction. Experiments are conducted on three public datasets. The average Pearson's linear correlation coefficient (PLCC) and Spearman's Rank Order Correlation Coefficient (SROCC) of the model we proposed reached 0.904 and 0.890, respectively. The experimental results verified that the proposed model has better performance compared with the current most mainstream methods.
