AB  - Sustainable fly ash cement bricks (FACBs) mitigate environmental degradation from fired clay bricks. However, sand-based FACBs exhibit high porosity and mix heterogeneity. This study proposes using stone dust to partially replace sand, addressing a literature gap by combining experiments with machine learning (ML) to predict compressive strength, environmental impact, and optimize design. The percentage ranges for constituents (fly ash (FA): 50–70 %, cement: 6–16 %, sand: 7–22 %, stone dust: 7–22 %) were determined through preliminary tests and a review of existing literature. This study investigated the FACB's compressive strength using 18 different mix proportions, evaluated through compression testing machine and interpreted using ML algorithms. After testing 450 water-immersed specimens cured for 7–120 days, the optimal mix achieved 14.9 Megapascal (MPa) compressive strength. Four ML algorithms, namely decision tree (DT), random forest (RF), extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), and light gradient boosting machine (LightGBM), were deployed to develop a predictive model. This investigation found that the developed XGBoost model demonstrated superior predictive performance. In addition, Shapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) were employed to identify the critical features that influence performance and to enhance model interpretability. Finally, the integration of metaheuristic optimization with ML algorithms enabled the multi-objective optimization of carbon emissions and compressive strength. Multi-objective optimization approach facilitated an 8 % enhancement in compressive strength under performance-maximized scenarios while concurrently yielding Pareto-optimal solutions that reduced carbon emissions by 31–35 % compared to conventional mixes, all while meeting required mechanical performance thresholds.
