AB  - The explosive growth of social networking platforms has generated vast amounts of user interactions and content, making group recommendation systems crucial for enhancing social interactions and group decision-making. However, traditional approaches depend on explicit ratings or user-item interactions, which are limited or absent in social contexts. To overcome shortcomings such as low accuracy, scalability issues, data sparsity, computational complexity, and overfitting, we propose the self-supervised social attentive deep reinforcement learning-based group recommender system (SSSADRGR). This system leverages rich social connections and contextual information within social networks to make personalized and group-aware recommendations. Operating in a self-supervised manner, it learns from inherent social interactions and group dynamics. The SSSADRGR model employs a hybrid deep generative adversarial network-based collaborative filtering, incorporating Self-Supervised Q-learning (SQN) and Self-Supervised Actor-Critics (SAC), with SAC networks designing the agent and DDPG optimizing the model. Experimental results on MovieLens and Amazon b datasets reveal significantly greater predictive performance compared to other approaches. The proposed model achieves 98 % accuracy, 97 % precision and F1 score, and 98 % recall. This methodology is applicable in online marketing, such as Amazon and Flipkart, for advertising purposes, effectively addressing key challenges in group recommendation systems while advancing the field through innovative integration of social attention and self-supervised learning.
