AB  - Although machine learning demonstrates significant advantages in predicting material properties, major bottlenecks still exist in the inverse design of novel materials with breakthrough performance. To address this issue, this paper proposes an inverse generative design framework that deeply integrates the global optimization mechanism of the simulated annealing algorithm with the category controllability of auxiliary classifier generative adversarial networks, establishing a dynamic balance mechanism for multi-objective cooperative optimization and single-target directed enhancement. By dynamically optimizing the latent space distribution through the random perturbations of the simulated annealing algorithm, the framework achieves targeted control of a specific property while ensuring excellent overall multi-objective performance. Taking the design of low-density and high-strength aluminum alloys for aerospace as an example, gradient boosting regression tree evaluation shows that the inverse generative design framework can generate novel aluminum alloy samples: while guaranteeing a specific strength greater than 142 megapascal cubic centimeters per gram, the average density can be reduced to 2.83 grams per cubic centimeter (a 3.1 percent decrease) or the average tensile strength enhanced to 621 megapascals (a 110.8 percent increase), verifying the dual advantages of multi-objective cooperation and single-target intensification, and providing an effective solution for efficient inverse material design.
