AB  - The increasing global production of waste rubber poses urgent environmental challenges, necessitating innovative solutions for its sustainable reuse. This study addresses this issue by integrating waste rubber into concrete as a partial aggregate substitute, combined with steel fibers to counteract strength reduction. The core novelty of this research lies in its development of a deep learning (DL)-driven approach to accurately predict the compressive strength of this rubber-steel fiber concrete, providing a transformative method for optimizing mix designs and advancing eco-friendly material development. Three machine learning models—eXtreme Gradient Boosting, Artificial Neural Network (ANN), and Long Short-Term Memory—were trained on a comprehensive dataset of 2200 experimental samples. The results demonstrate that ML models can predict concrete strength with high accuracy (error <3 %), effectively eliminating the reliance on traditional piecewise empirical models. The optimized ANN-based framework enables precise visualization of peak strength under varying rubber-steel fiber ratios, offering direct guidance for industrial practitioners. This methodology not only reduces the need for costly physical experiments but also establishes a scalable paradigm for waste repurposing in construction. The model's success is underscored by a 13 % strength improvement over conventional concrete, highlighting the potential of ML as a future-oriented tool that bridges environmental sustainability with industrial efficiency. Such data-driven strategies are poised to redefine resource utilization in the construction sector, fostering circular economies and accelerating the adoption of waste-derived composites.
