AB  - Data analysis in predictive maintenance (PdM) is changing industrial equipment management. In specific, machine learning algorithms can be useful for proactive industrial maintenance strategies focusing on predicting failures, planning actions to maximize equipment uptime. However, the class imbalance and the lack of interpretability hinder machine learning algorithms implementation in PdM. The objective of this research is to generate a model to accurately predict class imbalance datasets with a lack of interpretability verified in industrial water pump failures. A pipeline in three modules was developed based on data preprocessing, training and model selection, and model evaluation. The features are numerical signals obtained by sensors installed in the industrial water pump, and the target variable measures the machine's operational status. To generate models, the performance of three machine learning algorithms (random forest, support vector machine, and k-nearest neighbors) were explored with data sampling techniques to mitigate class imbalance, being validated through a 5-fold cross-validation process. Also, the feature importance analysis is conducted with Shapley additive explanations (SHAP) values to identify the positive or negative contributions of each feature. The best performance was obtained by combining random forest and ClusterCentroids, with the most important feature being sensor_04. Consequently, the model accurately predicts industrial water pump failures mainly when sensor_04 signals between 150 and 500 are recorded.
