AB  - Vehicle emissions significantly contribute to greenhouse gas emissions. Many studies on emissions have explored different powertrains, including internal combustion engine vehicles (ICE). Still, most have lacked an examination of vehicle emissions from hybrid powertrains, including Plug-in Hybrid Electric Vehicles (PHEV). Moreover, previous studies have primarily focused on carbon dioxide emissions, overlooking the impact of multiple pollutants, which remains a significant gap. This study effectively addressed the gap by utilizing a dataset of PHEV mode from a downloadable dynamometer database to establish a time series predictive model using an Autoencoder Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (AE-BiLSTM), which effectively deals with high noise and dimensions, capturing complex characteristics, non-linearity, and temporal dependencies. Moreover, the bidirectional structure enables the model to learn past and future data values sequentially, which is essential for hybrid engines, as these modes frequently switch between ICE and electric-powered battery modes. Firstly, the data is fed into an Autoencoder to extract fewer, yet essential, dimensions. Secondly, a Bidirectional Long Short-Term Model was employed to predict multiple pollutants of a selected vehicle. The predictive performance is also compared with other bidirectional and non-bidirectional approaches. The results revealed that the proposed model outperformed other models. Across all pollutants, AE-BiLSTM achieved an average R-Squared of 0.92, representing an improvement of approximately 2 % compared to selected bidirectional neural networks and 24 % compared to non-bidirectional approaches. The results indicate that bidirectional architectures significantly improved emission modeling of hybrid vehicles. This study presents a novel framework for predicting multiple pollutants in hybrid engines with enhanced accuracy.
