AB  - In recent years, the few-shot learning method based on causal theory has been gradually applied to the fault diagnosis of industrial equipment, and the interpretability and robustness of the model are improved. However, the existing few-shot learning methods based on causal theory mainly aim at the case where the confounders are observable, and these methods are not applicable to the more common case where the confounders are unobservable. In order to solve this problem, this paper proposes a prototype network based on front-door adjustment within an artificial intelligence framework for few-shot cross-component fault diagnosis, which achieves adjustment control of unobservable confounders by introducing mediating variables. Specifically, in this paper, the artificial intelligence–based dimension reduction and feature extraction of the input signal are firstly performed, and the prototype network is used to generate the prototype vectors of each category as intermediary variables. Then, the front-door adjustment module makes the causal relationship between input features and fault labels clearer by controlling the role of intermediary variables, and effectively eliminates the influence of unobservable confounders. On this basis, the Euclidean distance between the query sample and the support set prototype vector is calculated to realize the fault class recognition. Experimental results show that the proposed method outperforms the existing state-of-the-art methods in few-shot scenarios for artificial intelligence–enabled industrial fault diagnosis. Code is available at: https://github.com/llchen9426/FroDa-ProNet.
