AB  - Social media platforms must incorporate automated hate content detection since the propagation of such material can unnerve users and incite significant societal backlash. Memes, a form of multimedia content, are gaining considerable popularity online. Although the primary purpose of most memes is to entertain, some of them pose serious concerns by employing dark humour to spread hateful content. Motivated by the criticality of this problem and the scope for further exploration in multimodal hateful content detection, we propose a novel framework for identifying hateful content in memes consisting of both textual and visual content. The major contributions of this study are: (1) an attentional knowledge distillation-based textual feature extraction pipeline to acquire the vital information from the linguistic component of the meme samples; (2) a potent visual feature extractor based on a hierarchical vision transformer to help encapsulate the visual information from memes; (3) extensive experimental investigations conducted using three benchmark datasets, including Multi Modal Hate Speech Dataset, Multimodal Meme Dataset for Offensive Content, and Facebook's Hateful Memes Challenge; (4) superior outcomes (accuracy of 80.70 %, 62.85 %, and 87.80 % respectively on the three datasets) compared to cutting-edge methods when evaluated using various metrics; and (5) cross-dataset generalization study that establishes the ability of the proposed framework to detect hateful content in out-of-dataset samples. The efficacy of the proposed framework is further reinforced by additional experiments, such as ablation trials and qualitative visualization using class activation maps.
