AB  - Social media platforms are integral to modern communication, but are increasingly threatened by the proliferation of clone accounts. These fraudulent profiles facilitate the spread of misinformation, leading to significant social, commercial, and political consequences. Current automated methods for clone detection are largely inadequate. They predominantly rely on simple text or token matching, which fails to detect semantically equivalent code that has been structurally altered (e.g., through variable renaming), resulting in unacceptably high rates of false positives and negatives. This research aims to address this critical gap by developing a more robust and smart detection system. The proposed approach presents a deep learning-based framework that establishes a distinct neural network relationship across a targeted set of attributes. Our hybrid methodology integrates behavioral and user data analysis, where deep learning algorithms are employed to identify patterns and anomalies indicative of cloning, with network analysis that maps relationships between accounts to detect unusual activity patterns. In addition, cluster detection is applied to examine connection data and identify groups of related clone accounts, thereby improving detection accuracy. Our proposed approach outperforms traditional methods by capturing highly correlated features, enabling high-fidelity detection with scalability for large datasets and significantly lower false positive and negative rates. The effective handling of massive datasets through big data tools supports improved accuracy over time. A hybrid deep learning model combining semantic analysis and network relationships proves highly effective and scalable for smart clone account detection, guiding social media platforms toward stronger security, user protection, and network integrity.
