AB  - This paper investigates robust parameter identification for Hammerstein battery models by incorporating a radial basis function (RBF) neural network as the nonlinear block. By taking the RBF equation as the key term, the parameter identification difficulty caused by the product terms in the proposed model is solved. An innovative dynamic forgetting factor is introduced to solve a challenge with the fixed forgetting factor recursive stochastic gradient method, which has difficulty simultaneously maintaining a fast convergence rate and stability in battery parameter identification. This factor utilizes the residual between estimated and actual state of charge (SOC) values as a variable to formulate a correction formula to achieve the dynamic adjustment of forgetting factor. The maximum likelihood principle is used to derive a global optimal estimation method. The multi-innovation identification theory is introduced to further improve the SOC estimation accuracy. Experimental results demonstrate that the SOC steady-state error is within 0.5%. In addition, robustness and adaptability are verified under the LA92 working condition, with root mean square error (RMSE) and mean absolute error (MAE) values of 1.81010% and 0.045826%, respectively. The proposed modeling and identification methods can achieve accurate SOC estimation under various working conditions.
