AB  - Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) have become indispensable for detecting damage in civil structures. However, conventional gradient-based optimization methods are prone to issues like vanishing or exploding gradients, limiting their efficacy. In contrast, Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) offers a promising alternative by operating independently of gradients, ensuring robustness in challenging optimization scenarios. This study evaluates the performance of a CNN with the Adam optimizer against a PSO-optimized CNN in classifying seven damage classes in a finite-element two-span bridge model. Results averaged over 10 independent runs demonstrate that, despite identical architectures, employing PSO as a weight optimizer led to statistically significant improvements in damage detection accuracy. Specifically, the PSO-optimized CNN achieved final accuracies of 93.4 % and 85 % for seismic and service load scenarios respectively, representing enhancements of 6.43 % and 5 % over the Adam-optimized baseline. The findings demonstrate the superiority of PSO-optimized CNNs for seismic damage detection and provide a scalable framework for real-world Structural Health Monitoring (SHM) applications.
