AB  - Achieving economic and energy sustainability in manufacturing requires optimizing throughput and energy efficiency under machine unreliability. This study addresses the energy-efficient Buffer Allocation Problem using an adaptive Differential Evolution (DE) algorithm for both single- and multi-objective formulations. The framework couples the Equivalent Machine Method with a self-adaptive parameter control mechanism. The Golden Ratio principle is applied only to the single-objective case to minimize total buffer size while preserving desired throughput and energy levels. A knowledge-based initialization procedure generates informed initial solutions by distributing buffers according to machine-level effective rates, which accelerates convergence and improves stability compared to random initialization. Benchmark experiments on production lines with up to 50 machines show that the proposed adaptive DE algorithm outperforms classical heuristics in convergence speed and energy–throughput trade-offs. Sensitivity analysis identifies idle-state energy parameters as the dominant factor affecting total energy use. Computational profiling further shows that the proposed algorithm scales efficiently with CPU time while maintaining negligible memory growth. Validation on a real automatic stamping line confirms the industrial applicability and effectiveness of the method for designing sustainable, energy-aware production systems.
