AB  - Manufactured sand is a sustainable alternative to natural sand, but its angular shape and high stone powder content make it difficult to jointly meet workability, strength, and cost targets. This study addresses that bottleneck with an interpretable, goal-directed artificial intelligence (AI) workflow for concrete mix design. We adopt a filter-then-optimize pipeline that combines Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) for feature screening, a Particle Swarm Optimized Least-Squares Support Vector Machine (PSO-LSSVM) as a surrogate modeling, Non-dominated Sorting Genetic Algorithm II (NSGA-II) for Pareto search, and Technique for Order Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (TOPSIS) for target selection, which rapidly proposes mix proportions aligned with user-specified goals. Trained and cross-validated on a 715-sample database of manufactured-sand concretes, the framework concentrates the Pareto front around desired regions and makes trade-offs among slump, compressive strength, and unit cost transparent. Experiments on representative candidates validated the predictions, achieving 28-day strengths of 34–38 MPa (MPa) and slumps of 85–110 mm (mm) at competitive cost, with mean absolute relative errors ≤3 % (strength) and ≤5 % (slump). Sensitivity analyses confirmed the stability of selected variables and the robustness of the surrogate across realistic ranges of binder content, water-to-binder ratio, and fines content. By combining high-throughput screening with evolutionary optimization, the method returns compact, actionable solution sets and improves interpretability for practitioners. Overall, the workflow provides a reliable, transparent tool for performance-targeted, cost-effective mix design of manufactured-sand concrete.
