KW  - Brain–computer interface
KW  - Attention detection
AB  - Recent advancements in Electroencephalogram(EEG) based cognitive state decoding have emphasized the importance of capturing both spatial and temporal neural dynamics. However, existing methods often struggle to jointly preserve the topological structure of EEG channels and the evolving temporal dependencies of brain activity. To address these limitations, we propose a novel Topology-Constrained Hybrid Spatiotemporal Network (TCHS-Net), which integrates three key contribution: (1) introduce a parameter-free multidimensional attention module that enhances discriminative features without increasing computational cost, (2) a channel topology module that preserves the intrinsic structural consistency of EEG signals via topological correlations and one-dimensional convolutions, and (3) a temporal connectivity module that employs graph-based channel attention and sequence modeling to robustly capture time-varying dependencies. Validated on SEED-VIG, EEG-NIRS, and DREAMER datasets, TCHS-Net consistently surpasses state-of-the-art methods. Specifically, the accuracy rates for the fatigue state detection task and the attention detection task have increased by 3% and 5%, respectively, and the accuracy rate for the emotion recognition task has reached 88.49%, demonstrating strong generalization and computational efficiency. These results underscore the potential of TCHS-Net as a reliable instrument for decoding brain states in real-world brain–computer interface (BCI) applications.
