AB  - The temperature distribution in thermos-compression bonding electrodes is crucial for weld quality. However, capturing real-time images of temperature distribution in thermos-compression bonding electrodes under thermal fatigue damage remains a significant challenge. This paper presents a novel method for the real-time prediction of the temperature field in thermos-compression bonding electrodes experiencing thermal fatigue damage by integrating digital twin technology and a generative artificial intelligence model. Extended finite element simulation software is employed to construct a digital twin model of a healthy thermos-compression bonding electrode. The validity of the virtual electrode model is then verified through a comparison of the numerical simulation results of the electrode temperature field and experimental detection results. A self-updating strategy for the electrode's geometric model is proposed to construct a digital twin dataset representing the thermos-compression bonding electrode's temperature field across different thermal fatigue damage states. To address the issue of lengthy solution times in numerical simulations, an electrode temperature distribution image prediction model based on a conditional deep convolutional generative adversarial network is proposed. This model facilitates the rapid prediction of thermos-compression bonding electrode temperature distribution images and welding surface temperature curves across various damage states. Finally, the effectiveness and timeliness of the model's prediction results were analyzed. The findings demonstrate that the prediction method proposed in this paper accurately captures dynamic temperature changes in thermos-compression bonding electrodes undergoing thermal fatigue, with high fidelity and real-time performance. This provides a reliable foundation for future automated control of weld quality in thermos-compression bonding.
