AB  - In recent years, Remote Photoplethysmography (rPPG) technology has achieved significant advancements in non-contact health monitoring, although most existing methods still rely to some extent on labeled data. To alleviate this reliance, this study proposes a self-supervised contrastive learning framework for automatically extracting rPPG signals from facial videos. By generating spatiotemporal maps and utilizing a masked autoencoder pretraining Transformer architecture, we effectively capture the spatiotemporal dynamics within the video. Additionally, a novel loss function is proposed to enhance the consistency between the shape and peak frequency of the power spectral density. Experiments on several rPPG datasets demonstrate that the proposed method achieves accuracy on par with state-of-the-art supervised learning methods without the need for labeled data, showing strong robustness. This approach offers a fresh perspective for applying self-supervised learning in the rPPG domain and holds great potential for practical applications.
