AB  - This study introduces a convolutional neural network for predicting antenna operational frequency ranges from 256 × 256 binary maps representing pseudo-random two-dimensional designs with 50 vertices. Existing methods rely on conventional geometries, regression models, and expensive simulations, limiting scalability in early-stage design. The proposed approach formulates the task as a multi-label classification problem, allowing prediction of antenna operability without expert input or electromagnetic simulations. Key contributions include: (1) a novel framework for estimating operational ranges from binary maps of antenna topologies, (2) a new dataset of pseudo-random antenna topologies labeled with frequency ranges derived from simulations, (3) a convolutional neural network tailored to predicting the operational frequency range from binary maps, (4) experimental evaluation. The dataset includes 136,351 designs labeled across ten 0.5 GHz frequency bands (3–8 GHz). Samples may be assigned to multiple frequency bands simultaneously. Each of the ten output labels is equally represented with 15,000 samples, and 30,000 non-functional designs were included to enhance robustness. The proposed method enables rapid identification of promising designs for optimization. The model employs six convolutional and four fully connected layers optimized for high-dimensional binary inputs. It achieved a macro-averaged test accuracy of 0.9387 specificity of 0.9681, and a harmonic mean of precision and recall (F1 score) of 0.7015, outperforming other architectures in a directly comparable setting. Average inference time is 17.249 ms per design, significantly faster than electromagnetic simulations, making the method suitable for large-scale screening in automated design workflows. The dataset and trained model are publicly available to support reproducibility.
