AB  - State Estimation (SE) is a critical function in power grid operation, relying on the Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA) system to provide an accurate grid representation. However, SE is highly sensitive to anomalies (bad data), which can increase computation times and jeopardize real-time performance. Ensuring hard real-time requirements despite anomalies is essential for grid stability and reliability. This paper explores the use of Graph Deviation Networks (GDN) for anomaly detection in smart grid time-series data. GDN effectively captures both local and global dependencies in multivariate datasets, enabling early anomaly detection before impacting SE. The dynamic nature of smart grids leads to evolving anomalies, making fully labeled datasets impractical for supervised learning. Instead, semi-supervised learning reduces reliance on labeled data, offering a more adaptive and efficient detection approach. Results demonstrate that GDN outperforms conventional methods in both accuracy and robustness, enabling real-time anomaly filtering. By integrating machine learning-based anomaly detection, this study presents a practical approach to enhancing SE efficiency and ensuring the resilience of smart grid systems. The approach has been tested on three power systems, namely IEEE 6-bus, 14-bus, and 30-bus systems. F1 score of 98.39%, 98.88%, and 98.22% for IEEE 6-bus, 14-bus, and 30-bus systems, respectively, indicates excellent performance in detecting anomalies while minimizing false positives and false negatives. These metrics demonstrated that the GDN model is highly effective at detecting anomalies across various power grid datasets, significantly outperforming traditional methods. The proposed model introduces minimal computational overhead, with the prefiltering process taking less than 1% of total state estimation time. Despite this, it significantly enhances robustness by filtering out anomalous data before estimation, reducing convergence time by more than half. This improvement makes the approach particularly suitable for real-time power system monitoring and control.
