AB  - The integration of machine learning (ML) into thermal systems enables improved prediction accuracy, lower simulation costs, and enhanced engineering decision-making. This work explores three ML models: Support Vector Regression (SVR) with a Radial Basis Function (RBF) kernel, Random Forest (RF), and Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) to predict the thermal and energy performance of a small-scale solar chimney. Unlike most studies focused on large-scale systems, this research targets compact solar chimneys used in natural ventilation and agricultural drying, where experimental constraints limit predictive modeling. A dataset derived from validated mathematical and experimental models of a prototype in Belo Horizonte, Brazil, was used for training and evaluation. Results indicate that the MLP model outperforms the others in predicting airflow temperature and mass flow rate, achieving peak coefficients of determination (R2) above 0.999 for most variables. Moreover, the MLP exhibited the highest computational throughput, with approximately 6000 predictions per second, highlighting its suitability for real-time applications. Partial Dependence Plots (PDPs) were applied to interpret model behavior and assess the influence of ambient parameters. Overall, this study confirms the feasibility of ML for small-scale solar thermal applications and introduces a practical framework for predictive modeling, sensitivity analysis, and future real-time optimization.
