AB  - This study proposes a machine learning-based zero-shot learning cross-domain prediction method for offshore jacket platform structural health monitoring to address prediction challenges in non-measured regions caused by limited sensor deployment and data sparsity. The artificial intelligence (AI) framework integrates deep learning and ensemble learning techniques for mechanical response prediction, featuring three core components: (1) a residual learning-based panoramic prediction model that bridges simulation-measurement domain gaps by mapping environmental loads to structural responses; (2) a heterogeneous fusion mechanism integrating time-domain statistical features with wavelet-domain frequency features using information entropy optimization for adaptive feature weighting; and (3) a mutual information-based framework quantifying nonlinear dependencies between measurement points to enable zero-shot knowledge transfer. Validation on the LF15-1 offshore platform dataset demonstrates substantial performance improvements in axial force prediction applications under extreme marine conditions. The proposed AI-driven method achieves a maximum coefficient of determination (R2) value of 0.868 at critical unmeasured reference locations, representing up to 51.7 % improvement over Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) baselines. Compared to coarse-grained spatial extrapolation, zero-shot prediction accuracy improved by up to 41.0 %, with error reductions exceeding 35 %. Ablation studies confirm the necessity of each component, with individual removal causing R2 drops of 0.20–0.30 (corresponding to 30–70 % performance degradation relative to baseline methods), demonstrating their synergistic contribution to overall performance. The method demonstrates robust performance under extreme typhoon conditions, providing a practical AI-driven solution for marine structural safety assessment with sparse sensor data.
