AB  - The increasing complexity of global aviation security demands intelligent, scalable screening systems, especially for X-ray baggage threat detection. While deep learning (DL) and machine learning (ML) models have shown promise in automating such detection, their comparative evaluation remains fragmented due to inconsistent metric usage, varying metric importance, data variation, metric conflict, and uncertainty in expert judgment. To address these issues, this study proposes a hybrid decision-support framework based on Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM). The framework combines expert-driven and data-driven insights using a hybrid weighting approach that integrates Interval-Valued Spherical Fuzzy Sets with the Full Consistency Method (IvSFS–FUCOM) and Entropy. It applies four Grey Relational Coefficient (GRC)-based MCDM methods to rank models from multiple evaluation perspectives and uses the Rank Position Ensemble Method to derive a stable consensus ranking. Experiments were conducted on the DvXray dataset using 48 model combinations, including four deep feature extractors and twelve traditional classifiers, evaluated across twelve performance metrics. Results show that the combination of DenseNet121 with logistic regression consistently achieves top performance across all ranking stages. The proposed ensemble framework not only enhances ranking stability but also effectively addresses metric trade-offs, providing a robust, interpretable, and practically deployable decision-support tool for model selection in high-stakes aviation security environments.
