KW  - Physics-informed neural networks
KW  - Transformer
KW  - Cementing displacement
KW  - Multiphase flow modeling
KW  - Computational fluid dynamics
KW  - Shapley additive explanations
AB  - Cement slurry displacement efficiency directly determines oil and gas well cementing quality, yet accurate prediction remains challenging due to complex wellbore geometries, multiphase flow behaviors, and rheological variations. Conventional Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) simulations, although reliable, are computationally expensive and require repeated parameter adjustments, resulting in low efficiency and limited generalization. Purely data-driven models, by contrast, lack physical interpretability and often degrade when observational data are sparse. To address these issues, this study proposes a parameterized spatio-temporal Physics-Informed Neural Network (PINN) framework based on Kolmogorov–Arnold Networks (KAN)-Enhanced Transformer, applied here for the first time to cementing displacement efficiency. The framework incorporates continuity, Navier–Stokes, pressure Poisson, and phase-field constraints to ensure physical consistency. Its core network combines Transformer architecture with KAN, enabling mesh-free learning of nonlinear spatio-temporal features. Engineering validation demonstrates that the model reconstructs the displacement front with high accuracy within 20 s, much faster than CFD simulations. Across multiple metrics, it outperforms baseline Transformer and Multilayer Perceptron (MLP). Specifically, mean squared error (MSE) is reduced by 55.4 % and 84.7 %, while Spearman's rank correlation coefficient (SpearmanR) improves by 8.8 % and 5.1 % compared with Transformer and MLP, respectively. Furthermore, Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP) analysis highlights cement slurry density, rheological parameters, and pumping rate as dominant factors, providing interpretable insights for cement slurry design and operational optimization. This work advances artificial intelligence (AI) methodology through a novel parameterized PINN framework and demonstrates its engineering value in cementing.
